Early Pernicious Anaemia (Mild Macrocytosis)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Positive Intrinsic Factor antibodies, very mild fatigue, easily corrected with supplements

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-015
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe generalized weakness and dizziness requiring exclusion of profound anemia or acute neurological deficit.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset dizziness and subjective peripheral tingling in an elderly patient. Prompt differentiation between acute ischemic stroke, severe symptomatic macrocytic anemia requiring transfusion, or benign hyperventilation was strictly required to prevent catastrophic clinical delay prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I stood up and the room spun. My fingers are completely numb, and I have absolutely no energy to even lift my arms.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an elevated MCV with threshold B12 levels on a recent test. Tonight, the patient experienced a sudden episode of severe orthostatic dizziness followed by acute subjective weakness and bilateral finger paresthesia developing over 30 minutes. The patient recently read that pernicious anemia causes irreversible spinal cord damage, precipitating severe anxiety and secondary hyperventilation. Denies slurred speech, facial asymmetry, or true syncope.
Functional Status: Usually independent, currently refusing to mobilize due to fear of falling.
Social/Safety Concerns: High falls risk currently due to dizziness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/75 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-oriented, overtly anxious, breathing rapidly but with good color.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals normal cranial nerves, 5/5 power in all four limbs, and intact fine touch and proprioception globally, contradicting subjective numbness. Rhomberg test is negative. Positive Intrinsic Factor antibodies, very mild fatigue, easily corrected with supplements (history verified). Cardiovascular exam is normal. No postural blood pressure drop elicited on testing.
Environmental Observations: Clear living areas, mobility aids not required, safe environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications causing significant orthostasis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Anxiety/Hyperventilation mimicking neurological deficit in Early Pernicious Anaemia (Mild Macrocytosis) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke/TIA - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, symmetrical tingling rather than unilateral deficit, normal motor power, and normal cranial nerve exam.

Severe Symptomatic Anemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of resting tachycardia out of proportion to anxiety, no conjunctival pallor, and no postural hypotension.

Subacute Combined Degeneration of the Cord - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal proprioception, intact reflexes, and negative Romberg sign, confirming no actual posterior column involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute subjective weakness and bilateral paresthesia, raising a high clinical suspicion for an acute cerebrovascular event or severe symptomatic anemia requiring immediate transfusion. Triage accurately prioritized the case for an urgent neurological examination. Detailed bedside assessment definitively ruled out acute central or peripheral neurological deficits and severe anemia, identifying the symptoms as hyperventilation-induced paresthesia driven by anxiety over a new, mild diagnosis. Immediate clinical intervention was necessary to stabilize the patient, prevent a falls-risk cascade, and avoid an unnecessary emergency stroke call-out.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance and clear explanation of test results provided. Instructed on controlled breathing to resolve paresthesia.
Positive Behavioral Change: Breathe slowly in through your nose and out through pursed lips whenever you feel dizzy. By controlling your breathing rate, the tingling in your fingers will vanish entirely within five minutes, proving that your nerves are healthy and intact.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment within 7 days for B12 injection schedule planning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop sudden, true weakness or paralysis on one side of your body, slurred speech, or if you actually lose consciousness and fall.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Mild early pernicious anemia causes slow, mild fatigue. It does not cause sudden, acute neurological collapse. It is easily and completely treated with B12 injections.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the dizziness does not improve when you lie down flat, or if you develop crushing chest pain.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your tingling fingers were caused by breathing too fast due to panic, which temporarily changes the calcium levels in your blood. Your spinal cord is perfectly healthy.
Actionable Steps: Sit on the edge of the bed for a full 30 seconds before standing up to prevent normal dizziness. You will need a simple vitamin injection from your regular doctor.

